 field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=5,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186134135 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x = 1$, $x = 3$, $y =-1$, $y = 1$, $z = 0$, $z = 1$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F = xzi + y^{2}j + k$, the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = \\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}A A A A Adzdydx.\n$$\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?"] [17592186134136 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$"] [17592186134137 "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $F=x\\,i+xz\\,j+3z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186134138 "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,2y,\\,{x}^{2}+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$"] [17592186134139 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2xz\\,i+({x}^{2}+z)\\,j+2\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186134140 "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$"] [17592186134368 "Given that $F=2x{y}^{2}\\,i+4{x}^{2}y\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$"] [17592186134369 "Consider the vector field $F=xyz\\,i+{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,j+({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,0,0).$"] [17592186134370 "Given that $F(x, y, z) = xyi + yzj + xzk$, what is $curlF$?"] [17592186134371 "Consider the vector field $F = x\\cos yzi + y\\ln xzk$. Evaluate $∇ \\cdot F$ at the point $(1, \\frac{π}{2}, 1)$."] [17592186134376 "![](images/df/df940d9ea08ed1b326d4904933bd41c318112836d2d657abcb3c158aabbe7bb4.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$"] [17592186134377 "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation."] [17592186134378 "![](images/6f/6f51a25843259881994e3dca0781b780878bec349930b76fbf449dc824e69ec2.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*"] [17592186134386 "![](images/01/01a5f06674cacca4764f438a8c06f020659f0177a0b74952f40bc9072627ec3f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region."] [17592186167050 "Consider the vector field $F=(x-a){e}^{x+y}\\,i+x{e}^{x+y}\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$"] [17592186167090 "Consider the vector field $F=2ay\\,i+(3-x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$"] [17592186172280 "Determine the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$"] [17592186172281 "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$"] [17592186172301 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186172302 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=4x-3y.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(1,-3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186172303 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186172304 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨3{x}^{5}+2y,\\,3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,1),$ and $(-1,1).$"] [17592186172305 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+2y,\\,{x}^{2}+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$"] [17592186172306 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}+2{x}^{2}y,\\,{e}^{x}+2x{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,1),$ and $(-1,1).$"] [17592186172307 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186172308 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+1,\\,{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186172309 "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(2,0),(2,2),$ and $(-1,2),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}y\\,i+5xy\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186172675 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=xyz+{y}^{2}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,-1)$ to $(2,1,1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$"] [17592186176231 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,4-x{y}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ oriented counterclockwise."] [17592186176311 "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨x{y}^{2},\\,x+{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"] [17592186176313 "![](images/8b/8ba69d7bfe8dd363288bbf86661172004b21143f02c70260da0c184f066a7c12.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3},\\,y{e}^{y+1}+x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?\n$$"] [17592186176485 "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"] [17592186180029 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$"] [17592186180030 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+5\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$"] [17592186180038 "![](images/c7/c71395cfe5877546509f24656ba23f0883cbd3ee8c145c10abae9600c8bd5c4a.png)\n\nCalculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ through the curve $C$ given by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186180039 "![](images/27/27fe7019b715ca8f4d07a3f5e7f6ddc312288a135cb1dcb197aacbb98765cdf9.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by ${y}^{2}=4x$ from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(1,2).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\,i-4\\,j$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $n$ on $C,$ as shown above."] [17592186180040 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(y-x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$"] [17592186180041 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i+xy\\,j$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.$"] [17592186198593 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z-y,\\,2y-z,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ and $-2\\le y\\le 3.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186198594 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z+y,\\,2y+z,\\,xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$"] [17592186198602 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186198605 "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?"] [17592186198610 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨{x}^{2},\\,{y}^{2},\\,{z}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?"] [17592186198615 "Calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{e}^{x},\\,{e}^{y},\\,z+1⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186234248 "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right),\\,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0$ | $b$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $a$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $-a+b+c.$"] [17592186235522 "The divergence $\\text{div}\\,V$ of the velocity field represents the scalar rate of expansion of the fluid per unit of area at a given point. Find the corresponding expansion rate at the point $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nThe required rate of expansion is $\\text{div}\\,V\\,{|}_{(1,\\,1)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186235527 "The scalar curl of a plane velocity field is called the **vorticity,** denoted $\\omega .$ The vorticity describes a measure of the local rotation of the field at a given point. Find the vorticity of $V$ at $(1,1).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\omega (1,1)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ."]}
